an8.11:2.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama, I have heard that | T: “I have heard, Master Gotama:
an8.11:2.2 | S: the ascetic Gotama doesn’t bow to old brahmins, the elderly and senior, who are advanced in years and have reached the final stage of life; nor does he rise in their presence or offer them a seat. | T: ‘The ascetic Gotama does not pay homage to brahmins who are old, aged, burdened with years, advanced in life, come to the last stage; nor does he stand up for them or offer them a seat.’
an8.11:2.3 | S: And this is indeed the case, | T: This is indeed true,
an8.11:2.4 | S: for the worthy Gotama does not bow to old brahmins, elderly and senior, who are advanced in years and have reached the final stage of life; nor does he rise in their presence or offer them a seat. | T: for Master Gotama does not pay homage to brahmins who are old, aged, burdened with years, advanced in life, come to the last stage; nor does he stand up for them or offer them a seat.
an8.11:2.5 | S: This is not appropriate, worthy Gotama.” | T: This is not proper, Master Gotama.”
an8.11:2.6 | S: “Brahmin, I don’t see anyone in this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—for whom I should bow down or rise up or offer a seat. | T: “Brahmin, in the world with its devas, Māra, and Brahmā, in this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its devas and humans, I do not see one to whom I should pay homage, or for whom I should stand up, or whom I should offer a seat.
an8.11:2.7 | S: If the Realized One bowed down or rose up or offered a seat to anyone, their head would explode!” | T: For if the Tathāgata should pay homage to anyone, or stand up for him, or offer him a seat, even that person’s head would split.”
an8.11:3.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama lacks taste.” | T: (1) “Master Gotama lacks taste.”
an8.11:3.2 | S: “There is, brahmin, a sense in which you could rightly say | T: “There is, brahmin, a way in which one could rightly say of me:
an8.11:3.3 | S: that I lack taste. | T: ‘The ascetic Gotama lacks taste.’
an8.11:3.4 | S: For the Realized One has given up taste for sights, sounds, smells, tastes, and touches. It’s cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, obliterated, and unable to arise in the future. | T: The Tathāgata has abandoned his taste for forms, sounds, odors, tastes, and tactile objects; he has cut it off at the root, made it like a palm stump, obliterated it so that it is no more subject to future arising.
