an8.11:8.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama is an exterminator.” | T: (6) “Master Gotama is an abolitionist.”
an8.11:8.2 | S: “There is, brahmin, a sense in which you could rightly say that | T: “There is, brahmin, a way in which one could rightly say of me:
an8.11:8.3 | S: I’m an exterminator. | T: ‘The ascetic Gotama is an abolitionist.’
an8.11:8.4 | S: For I teach the extermination of greed, hate, and delusion, and the many kinds of unskillful things. | T: For I teach the Dhamma for the abolition of lust, hatred, and delusion; I teach the Dhamma for the abolition of the numerous kinds of bad unwholesome qualities.
an8.11:8.5 | S: In this sense you could rightly say that I’m an exterminator. | T: It is in this way that one could rightly say of me: ‘The ascetic Gotama is an abolitionist.’
an8.11:8.6 | S: But that’s not what you’re talking about.” | T: But you did not speak with reference to this.”
an8.11:9.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama is a mortifier.” | T: (7) “Master Gotama is a tormentor.”
an8.11:9.2 | S: “There is, brahmin, a sense in which you could rightly say that | T: “There is, brahmin, a way in which one could rightly say of me:
an8.11:9.3 | S: I’m a mortifier. | T: ‘The ascetic Gotama is a tormentor.’
an8.11:9.4 | S: For I say that bad conduct by way of body, speech, and mind should be mortified. | T: For I assert that bad unwholesome qualities—bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct—are to be burned up.
an8.11:9.5 | S: I say that a mortifier is someone who has given up unskillful qualities that should be mortified. They’ve cut them off at the root, made them like a palm stump, obliterated them, so that they’re unable to arise in the future. | T: I say that someone is a tormentor when he has abandoned the bad unwholesome qualities that are to be burned up; when he has cut them off at the root, made them like a palm stump, obliterated them so that they are no more subject to future arising.
an8.11:9.6 | S: The Realized One is someone who has given up unskillful qualities that should be mortified. He has cut them off at the root, made them like a palm stump, obliterated them, so that they’re unable to arise in the future. | T: The Tathāgata has abandoned the bad unwholesome qualities that are to be burned up; he has cut them off at the root, made them like a palm stump, obliterated them so that they are no more subject to future arising.
an8.11:9.7 | S: In this sense you could rightly say that I’m a mortifier. | T: It is in this way that one could rightly say of me: ‘The ascetic Gotama is a tormentor.’
an8.11:9.8 | S: But that’s not what you’re talking about.” | T: But you did not speak with reference to this.”
an8.11:10.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama is gutless.” | T: (8) “Master Gotama is retiring.”
an8.11:10.2 | S: “There is, brahmin, a sense in which you could rightly say that | T: “There is, brahmin, a way in which one could rightly say of me:
an8.11:10.3 | S: I am gutless. | T: ‘The ascetic Gotama is retiring.’
an8.11:10.4 | S: I say that a gutless person is someone who has given up future wombs and regeneration into a new state of existence. They’ve cut them off at the root, made them like a palm stump, obliterated them, so that they’re unable to arise in the future. | T: For I say that someone is retiring when he has abandoned the production of renewed existence, the future bed of the womb; when he has cut it off at the root, made it like a palm stump, obliterated it so that it is no more subject to future arising.
an8.11:10.5 | S: The Realized One has given up future wombs and regeneration into a new state of existence. He has cut them off at the root, made them like a palm stump, obliterated them, so that they’re unable to arise in the future. | T: The Tathāgata has abandoned the production of renewed existence, the future bed of the womb; he has cut it off at the root, made it like a palm stump, obliterated it so that it is no more subject to future arising.
an8.11:10.6 | S: In this sense you could rightly say that I am gutless. | T: It is in this way that one could rightly say of me: ‘The ascetic Gotama is retiring.’
an8.11:10.7 | S: But that’s not what you’re talking about. | T: But you did not speak with reference to this.
an8.11:11.1 | S: Suppose, brahmin, there was a chicken with eight or ten or twelve eggs. | T: “Suppose, brahmin, there was a hen with eight, ten, or twelve eggs
an8.11:11.2 | S: And she properly sat on them to keep them warm and incubated. | T: that she had properly covered, incubated, and nurtured.
an8.11:11.3 | S: Now, the chick that is first to break out of the eggshell with its claws and beak and hatch safely: should that be called the eldest or the youngest?” | T: Should the first among those chicks to pierce its shell with the points of its claws or beak and safely hatch be called the eldest or the youngest?”
an8.11:11.4 | S: “Worthy Gotama, that should be called the eldest. For it is the eldest among them.” | T: “It should be called the eldest, Master Gotama. So it is the eldest among them.”

