 succeeded in 0ms:
an8.11:12.1 | S: “In the same way, in this population lost in ignorance, swaddled in their shells, I alone have broken open the egg of ignorance and realized the supreme perfect awakening. | T: “So too, brahmin, in a population immersed in ignorance, become like an egg, completely enveloped, I have pierced the eggshell of ignorance. I am the sole person in the world who has awakened to the unsurpassed perfect enlightenment.
an8.11:12.2 | S: So, brahmin, I am the eldest and the first in the world. | T: So I am the eldest, the best in the world.
an8.11:13.1 | S: My energy was roused up and unflagging, my mindfulness was established and lucid, my body was tranquil and undisturbed, and my mind was immersed and unified in samādhi. | T: “My energy, brahmin, was aroused without slackening; my mindfulness was established without confusion; my body was tranquil without disturbance; my mind was concentrated and one-pointed.
an8.11:13.2 | S: Quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, I entered and remained in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: Secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unwholesome states, I entered and dwelled in the first jhāna, which consists of rapture and pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by thought and examination.
an8.11:13.3 | S: As the placing of the mind and keeping it connected were stilled, I entered and remained in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: With the subsiding of thought and examination, I entered and dwelled in the second jhāna, which has internal placidity and unification of mind and consists of rapture and pleasure born of concentration, without thought and examination.
an8.11:13.4 | S: And with the fading away of rapture, I entered and remained in the third absorption, where I meditated with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss.’ | T: With the fading away as well of rapture, I dwelled equanimous and, mindful and clearly comprehending, I experienced pleasure with the body; I entered and dwelled in the third jhāna of which the noble ones declare: ‘He is equanimous, mindful, one who dwells happily.’
an8.11:13.5 | S: With the giving up of pleasure and pain, and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, I entered and remained in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness. | T: With the abandoning of pleasure and pain, and with the previous passing away of joy and dejection, I entered and dwelled in the fourth jhāna, neither painful nor pleasant, which has purification of mindfulness by equanimity.
an8.11:14.1 | S: When my mind had immersed in samādhi like this—purified, bright, flawless, rid of corruptions, pliable, workable, steady, and imperturbable—I extended it toward recollection of past lives. | T: “When my mind was thus concentrated, purified, cleansed, unblemished, rid of defilement, malleable, wieldy, steady, and attained to imperturbability, I directed it to the knowledge of the recollection of past abodes.
an8.11:14.2 | S: I recollected many kinds of past lives. That is: one, two, three, four, five, ten, twenty, thirty, forty, fifty, a hundred, a thousand, a hundred thousand rebirths; many eons of the world contracting, many eons of the world expanding, many eons of the world contracting and expanding. I remembered: ‘There, I was named this, my clan was that, I looked like this, and that was my food. This was how I felt pleasure and pain, and that was how my life ended. When I passed away from that place I was reborn somewhere else. There, too, I was named this, my clan was that, I looked like this, and that was my food. This was how I felt pleasure and pain, and that was how my life ended. When I passed away from that place I was reborn here.’ And so I recollected my many kinds of past lives, with features and details. | T: I recollected my manifold past abodes, that is, one birth, two births, three births, four births, five births, ten births, twenty births, thirty births, forty births, fifty births, a hundred births, a thousand births, a hundred thousand births, many eons of world-dissolution, many eons of world-evolution, many eons of world-dissolution and world-evolution thus: ‘There I was so named, of such a clan, with such an appearance, such was my food, such my experience of pleasure and pain, such my life span; passing away from there, I was reborn elsewhere, and there too I was so named, of such a clan, with such an appearance, such was my food, such my experience of pleasure and pain, such my life span; passing away from there, I was reborn here.’ Thus I recollected my manifold past abodes with their aspects and details.
an8.11:15.1 | S: This was the first knowledge, which I achieved in the first watch of the night. | T: “This, brahmin, was the first true knowledge attained by me in the first watch of the night.
an8.11:15.2 | S: Ignorance was banished and knowledge arose; darkness was banished and light arose, as happens for a meditator who is diligent, keen, and resolute. | T: Ignorance was dispelled, true knowledge had arisen; darkness was dispelled, light had arisen, as happens when one dwells heedful, ardent, and resolute.
an8.11:15.3 | S: This was my first breaking out, like a chick breaking out of the eggshell. | T: This, brahmin, was my first breaking out, like that of the chick breaking out of the eggshell.
an8.11:16.1 | S: When my mind had immersed in samādhi like this—purified, bright, flawless, rid of corruptions, pliable, workable, steady, and imperturbable—I extended it toward knowledge of the death and rebirth of sentient beings. | T: “When my mind was thus concentrated, purified, cleansed, unblemished, rid of defilement, malleable, wieldy, steady, and attained to imperturbability, I directed it to the knowledge of the passing away and rebirth of beings.
an8.11:16.2 | S: With clairvoyance that is purified and superhuman, I saw sentient beings passing away and being reborn—inferior and superior, beautiful and ugly, in a good place or a bad place. I understood how sentient beings pass on according to their deeds: ‘These dear beings did bad things by way of body, speech, and mind. They denounced the noble ones; they had wrong view; and they chose to act out of that wrong view. When their body broke up, after death, they were reborn in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. These dear beings, however, did good things by way of body, speech, and mind. They never denounced the noble ones; they had right view; and they chose to act out of that right view. When their body broke up, after death, they were reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm.’ And so, with clairvoyance that is purified and superhuman, I saw sentient beings passing away and being reborn—inferior and superior, beautiful and ugly, in a good place or a bad place. I understood how sentient beings pass on according to their deeds. | T: With the divine eye, which is purified and surpasses the human, I saw beings passing away and being reborn, inferior and superior, beautiful and ugly, fortunate and unfortunate, and I understood how beings fare in accordance with their kamma thus: ‘These beings who engaged in misconduct by body, speech, and mind, who reviled the noble ones, held wrong view, and undertook kamma based on wrong view, with the breakup of the body, after death, have been reborn in the plane of misery, in a bad destination, in the lower world, in hell; but these beings who engaged in good conduct by body, speech, and mind, who did not revile the noble ones, who held right view, and undertook kamma based on right view, with the breakup of the body, after death, have been reborn in a good destination, in the heavenly world.’ Thus with the divine eye, which is purified and surpasses the human, I saw beings passing away and being reborn, inferior and superior, beautiful and ugly, fortunate and unfortunate, and I understood how beings fare in accordance with their kamma.
an8.11:17.1 | S: This was the second knowledge, which I achieved in the middle watch of the night. | T: “This, brahmin, was the second true knowledge attained by me in the middle watch of the night.
an8.11:17.2 | S: Ignorance was banished and knowledge arose; darkness was banished and light arose, as happens for a meditator who is diligent, keen, and resolute. | T: Ignorance was dispelled, true knowledge had arisen; darkness was dispelled, light had arisen, as happens when one dwells heedful, ardent, and resolute.
an8.11:17.3 | S: This was my second breaking out, like a chick breaking out of the eggshell. | T: This, brahmin, was my second breaking out, like that of the chick breaking out of the eggshell.
an8.11:18.1 | S: When my mind had immersed in samādhi like this—purified, bright, flawless, rid of corruptions, pliable, workable, steady, and imperturbable—I extended it toward knowledge of the ending of defilements. | T: “When my mind was thus concentrated, purified, cleansed, unblemished, rid of defilement, malleable, wieldy, steady, and attained to imperturbability, I directed it to the knowledge of the destruction of the taints.
an8.11:18.2 | S: I truly understood: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: I understood as it really is: ‘This is suffering’; I understood as it really is: ‘This is the origin of suffering’; I understood as it really is: ‘This is the cessation of suffering’; I understood as it really is: ‘This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering.’
an8.11:18.3 | S: I truly understood: ‘These are defilements’ … ‘This is the origin of defilements’ … ‘This is the cessation of defilements’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of defilements’. | T: I understood as it really is: ‘These are the taints’; I understood as it really is: ‘This is the origin of the taints’; I understood as it really is: ‘This is the cessation of the taints’; I understood as it really is: ‘This is the way leading to the cessation of the taints.’
an8.11:18.4 | S: Knowing and seeing like this, my mind was freed from the defilements of sensuality, desire to be reborn, and ignorance. | T: “When I knew and saw thus, my mind was liberated from the taint of sensuality, from the taint of existence, and from the taint of ignorance.
an8.11:18.5 | S: When it was freed, I knew it was freed. | T: When it was liberated there came the knowledge: ‘It’s liberated.’
an8.11:18.6 | S: I understood: ‘Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: I directly knew: ‘Destroyed is birth, the spiritual life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, there is no more coming back to any state of being.’
an8.11:19.1 | S: This was the third knowledge, which I achieved in the last watch of the night. | T: “This, brahmin, was the third true knowledge attained by me in the last watch of the night.
an8.11:19.2 | S: Ignorance was banished and knowledge arose; darkness was banished and light arose, as happens for a meditator who is diligent, keen, and resolute. | T: Ignorance was dispelled, true knowledge had arisen; darkness was dispelled, light had arisen, as happens when one dwells heedful, ardent, and resolute.
an8.11:19.3 | S: This was my third breaking out, like a chick breaking out of the eggshell.” | T: This, brahmin, was my third breaking out, like that of the chick breaking out of the eggshell.”
an8.11:20.1 | S: When he said this, the brahmin Verañja said to the Buddha: | T: When this was said, the brahmin of Verañjā said to the Blessed One:
an8.11:20.2 | S: “The worthy Gotama is the eldest! The worthy Gotama is the best! | T: “Master Gotama is the eldest! Master Gotama is the best!
an8.11:20.3 | S: Excellent, worthy Gotama! Excellent! | T: Excellent, Master Gotama! Excellent, Master Gotama!
an8.11:20.4 | S: As if he were righting the overturned, or revealing the hidden, or pointing out the path to the lost, or lighting a lamp in the dark so people with clear eyes can see what’s there, worthy Gotama has made the teaching clear in many ways. | T: Master Gotama has made the Dhamma clear in many ways, as though he were turning upright what had been overthrown, revealing what was hidden, showing the way to one who was lost, or holding up a lamp in the darkness so those with good eyesight can see forms.
an8.11:20.5 | S: I go for refuge to the worthy Gotama, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: I now go for refuge to Master Gotama, to the Dhamma, and to the Saṅgha of bhikkhus.
an8.11:20.6 | S: From this day forth, may the worthy Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: Let Master Gotama consider me a lay follower who from today has gone for refuge for life.”
an8.11:20.7 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.120
an8.120:1.1 | S: “For insight into greed, eight things should be developed. | T: “Bhikkhus, for direct knowledge of lust, eight things are to be developed.
an8.120:1.2 | S: What eight? | T: What eight?
an8.120:1.3 | S: Having physical form, they see forms … not perceiving form internally, they see forms externally … they’re focused only on beauty … going totally beyond perceptions of form, with the disappearance of perceptions of impingement, not focusing on perceptions of diversity, aware that ‘space is infinite’, they enter and remain in the dimension of infinite space … going totally beyond the dimension of infinite space, aware that ‘consciousness is infinite’, they enter and remain in the dimension of infinite consciousness … going totally beyond the dimension of infinite consciousness, aware that ‘there is nothing at all’, they enter and remain in the dimension of nothingness … going totally beyond the dimension of nothingness, they enter and remain in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception … going totally beyond the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, they enter and remain in the cessation of perception and feeling … | T: (1) One possessing form sees forms. (2) One not percipient of form internally sees forms externally. (3) One is focused only on ‘beautiful.’ (4) With the complete surmounting of perceptions of forms, with the passing away of perceptions of sensory impingement, with non-attention to perceptions of diversity, perceiving ‘space is infinite,’ one enters and dwells in the base of the infinity of space. (5) By completely surmounting the base of the infinity of space, perceiving ‘consciousness is infinite,’ one enters and dwells in the base of the infinity of consciousness. (6) By completely surmounting the base of the infinity of consciousness, perceiving ‘there is nothing,’ one enters and dwells in the base of nothingness. (7) By completely surmounting the base of nothingness, one enters and dwells in the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception. (8) By completely surmounting the base of neither-perceptionnor-non-perception, one enters and dwells in the cessation of perception and feeling.
an8.120:1.4 | S: For insight into greed, these eight things should be developed.” | T: For direct knowledge of lust, these eight things are to be developed.”

## an8.121-147
an8.121-147:1.1 | S: “For the complete understanding of greed … | T: “Bhikkhus, for full understanding of lust …
an8.121-147:1.2 | S: complete ending … | T: for the utter destruction …
an8.121-147:1.3 | S: giving up … | T: for the abandoning …
an8.121-147:1.4 | S: ending … | T: for the destruction …
an8.121-147:1.5 | S: vanishing … | T: for the vanishing …
an8.121-147:1.6 | S: fading away … | T: for the fading away …
an8.121-147:1.7 | S: cessation … | T: for the cessation …
an8.121-147:1.8 | S: giving away … | T: for the giving up …
an8.121-147:1.9 | S: letting go … | T: for the relinquishment of lust …
an8.121-147:1.10 | S: these eight things should be developed.” | T: these eight things are to be developed.”

## an8.13
an8.13:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a fine royal thoroughbred with eight factors is worthy of a king, fit to serve a king, and reckoned a factor of kingship. | T: “Bhikkhus, possessing eight factors, a king’s excellent thoroughbred horse is worthy of a king, an accessory of a king, and reckoned as a factor of kingship.
an8.13:1.2 | S: What eight? | T: What eight?
an8.13:1.3 | S: It’s when a fine royal thoroughbred is well born on both | T: “Here, (1) a king’s excellent thoroughbred horse is well born on both sides,
an8.13:1.4 | S: the mother’s and the father’s sides. | T: maternal and paternal;
an8.13:1.5 | S: He’s bred in the region fine thoroughbreds come from. | T: he is born in whatever area other excellent thoroughbred horses are born.
an8.13:1.6 | S: Whatever food he’s given, | T: (2) He respectfully eats whatever food they give him,
an8.13:1.7 | S: fresh or dry, | T: whether moist or dry,
an8.13:1.8 | S: he eats carefully, without making a mess. | T: without scattering it.
an8.13:1.9 | S: He’s disgusted by sitting or lying down in excrement or urine. | T: (3) He is repelled by sitting down or lying down near feces or urine.
an8.13:1.10 | S: He’s gentle and pleasant to live with, and he doesn’t upset the other horses. | T: (4) He is mild and pleasant to live with, and he does not agitate other horses.
an8.13:1.11 | S: He openly shows his tricks, bluffs, ruses, and feints to his trainer, | T: (5) He reveals his tricks, ploys, gambits, and wiles as they really are to his trainer
an8.13:1.12 | S: so the trainer can try to subdue them. | T: so that his trainer can make an effort to stamp them out of him.
an8.13:1.13 | S: He carries his load, determining: | T: (6) He carries loads, determined:
an8.13:1.14 | S: ‘Whether or not the other horses carry their loads, I’ll carry mine.’ | T: ‘Whether or not the other horses carry loads, I myself will carry them.’
an8.13:1.15 | S: He always walks in a straight path. | T: (7) When moving, he moves only along a straight path.
an8.13:1.16 | S: He’s strong, and stays strong even until death. | T: (8) He is strong, and he shows his strength right up until the end of his life.
an8.13:1.17 | S: A fine royal thoroughbred with these eight factors is worthy of a king. … | T: Possessing these eight factors, a king’s excellent thoroughbred horse is worthy of a king, an accessory of a king, and reckoned as a factor of kingship.
an8.13:2.1 | S: In the same way, a mendicant with eight qualities is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of greeting with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world. | T: “So too, bhikkhus, possessing eight qualities, a bhikkhu is worthy of gifts … an unsurpassed field of merit for the world.
an8.13:2.2 | S: What eight? | T: What eight?
an8.13:2.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: (1) “Here, a bhikkhu is virtuous; he dwells restrained by the Pātimokkha, possessed of good conduct and resort, seeing danger in minute faults. Having undertaken the training rules, he trains in them.
an8.13:2.4 | S: Whatever food they’re given, | T: (2) He respectfully eats whatever food they give him,
an8.13:2.5 | S: coarse or fine, | T: whether coarse or excellent,
an8.13:2.6 | S: they eat carefully, without bother. | T: without being annoyed.
an8.13:2.7 | S: They’re disgusted with bad conduct by way of body, speech, or mind, | T: (3) He is repelled by bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct;
an8.13:2.8 | S: and by attainment of the many kinds of unskillful things. | T: he is repelled by the acquisition of the numerous kinds of bad unwholesome qualities.
an8.13:2.9 | S: They’re gentle and pleasant to live with, and they don’t upset the other mendicants. | T: (4) He is mild and pleasant to live with, and he does not agitate other bhikkhus.
an8.13:2.10 | S: They openly show their tricks, bluffs, ruses, and feints to their sensible spiritual companions, | T: (5) He reveals his tricks, ploys, gambits, and wiles as they really are to the Teacher or to his wise fellow monks
an8.13:2.11 | S: so they can try to subdue them. | T: so that they can make an effort to stamp them out of him.
an8.13:2.12 | S: They do their training, determining: | T: (6) He is one who takes up the training, determined:
an8.13:2.13 | S: ‘Whether or not the other mendicants do their training, I’ll do mine.’ | T: ‘Whether or not other bhikkhus train, I will train.’
an8.13:2.14 | S: They always walk in a straight path. | T: (7) When moving, he moves only along a straight path.
an8.13:2.15 | S: And here the straight path is | T: In this connection, this is the straight path:
an8.13:2.16 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view … right concentration.
an8.13:2.17 | S: They’re energetic: | T: (8) He has aroused energy thus:
an8.13:2.18 | S: ‘Gladly, let my skin, sinews, and bones remain! Let the blood and flesh waste away in my body! I will not stop trying until I have achieved what is possible by human strength, energy, and vigor.’ | T: ‘Willingly, let only my skin, sinews, and bones remain, and let the flesh and blood dry up in my body, but I will not relax my energy so long as I have not attained what can be attained by manly strength, energy, and exertion.’
an8.13:2.19 | S: A mendicant with these eight qualities is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of veneration with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world.” | T: Possessing these eight qualities, a bhikkhu is worthy of gifts … an unsurpassed field of merit for the world.”
an8.13:2.20 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.14
an8.14:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you about eight wild colts and eight defects in horses, and about eight wild people and eight defects in people. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you the eight kinds of wild colts and the eight faults of a horse, and I will teach you the eight kinds of persons who are like wild colts and the eight faults of a person.
an8.14:1.2 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen and attend closely. I will speak.”
an8.14:1.3 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, Bhante,” those bhikkhus replied.
an8.14:1.4 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
an8.14:2.1 | S: “And what, mendicants, are the eight wild colts and eight defects in horses? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, are the eight kinds of wild colts and the eight faults of a horse?
an8.14:2.2 | S: Firstly, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts back right up and spin the chariot behind them. | T: (1) “Here, when a wild colt is told: ‘Go forward!’ and is being spurred and incited by its trainer, it backs up and spins the chariot around behind it.
an8.14:2.3 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: There is such a kind of wild colt here.
an8.14:2.4 | S: This is the first defect of a horse. | T: This is the first fault of a horse.
an8.14:3.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts jump back, wreck the hub, and break the triple rod. | T: (2) “Again, when a wild colt is told: ‘Go forward!’ and is being spurred and incited by its trainer, it leaps back and thereby damages the rail and breaks the triple rod.
an8.14:3.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: There is such a kind of wild colt here.
an8.14:3.3 | S: This is the second defect of a horse. | T: This is the second fault of a horse.
an8.14:4.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts shake the draft-pole off their thigh and trample it. | T: (3) “Again, when a wild colt is told: ‘Go forward!’ and is being spurred and incited by its trainer, it loosens its thigh from the chariot pole and crushes the chariot pole.
an8.14:4.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: There is such a kind of wild colt here.
an8.14:4.3 | S: This is the third defect of a horse. | T: This is the third fault of a horse.
an8.14:5.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts take a wrong turn, sending the chariot off track. | T: (4) “Again, when a wild colt is told: ‘Go forward!’ and is being spurred and incited by its trainer, it takes a wrong path and leads the chariot off the track.
an8.14:5.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: There is such a kind of wild colt here.
an8.14:5.3 | S: This is the fourth defect of a horse. | T: This is the fourth fault of a horse.
an8.14:6.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts rear up and strike out with their fore-legs. | T: (5) “Again, when a wild colt is told: ‘Go forward!’ and is being spurred and incited by its trainer, it leaps up with the front of its body and churns the air with its front feet.
an8.14:6.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: There is such a kind of wild colt here.
an8.14:6.3 | S: This is the fifth defect of a horse. | T: This is the fifth fault of a horse.
an8.14:7.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts ignore the trainer and the goad, spit out the bit, and go wherever they want. | T: (6) “Again, when a wild colt is told: ‘Go forward!’ and is being spurred and incited by its trainer, it does not heed its trainer or the goad but destroys the mouthbit with its teeth and sets out wherever it wishes.
an8.14:7.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: There is such a kind of wild colt here.
an8.14:7.3 | S: This is the sixth defect of a horse. | T: This is the sixth fault of a horse.
an8.14:8.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts don’t step forward or turn back but stand right there still as a post. | T: (7) “Again, when a wild colt is told: ‘Go forward!’ and is being spurred and incited by its trainer, it does not go forward or turn back but stands right there as still as a post.
an8.14:8.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: There is such a kind of wild colt here.
an8.14:8.3 | S: This is the seventh defect of a horse. | T: This is the seventh fault of a horse.
an8.14:9.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts tuck in their fore-legs and hind-legs, and sit right down on their four legs. | T: (8) “Again, when a wild colt is told: ‘Go forward!’ and is being spurred and incited by its trainer, it tucks in its front legs and its back legs and sits down right there on all four legs.
an8.14:9.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: There is such a kind of wild colt here.
an8.14:9.3 | S: This is the eighth defect of a horse. | T: This is the eighth fault of a horse.
an8.14:9.4 | S: These are the eight wild colts and the eight defects in horses. | T: “These are the eight kinds of wild colts and the eight faults of a horse.
an8.14:10.1 | S: And what are the eight wild people and eight defects in people? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, are the eight kinds of persons who are like wild colts and the eight faults of a person?
an8.14:10.2 | S: Firstly, the mendicants accuse a mendicant of an offense. | T: (1) “Here, when the bhikkhus are reproving a bhikkhu for an offense,
